course: dictionary
lesson: 12308
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. «The corporation makes cars and phones in factories all over the world.» The words cars, phones and factories show a big company, not a town or a belly.
explain: «The corporation voted to repair the park's swings.» Now the words park and swings show a group of people who run a town. The same word has a different meaning because the clues are different.
explain: Ask yourself which meaning fits the sentence, then swap it in and check that it still makes sense. A dictionary lists the meanings in numbered order, so you can try each one until the sentence works.
fact: A corporation is a large company or group of companies that the law treats as one body.
fact: In the UK, a corporation can also be the town council, the group of people who run a town or city.
fact: An old-fashioned, humorous meaning is a big round belly. You will rarely see this today.
fact: The BBC stands for the British Broadcasting Corporation.
fact: Big corporations make cars, phones and food, and they run factories around the world.
exercise: «The corporation makes cars and phones in factories all over the world.» What is a corporation here? | *A large company | A kind of cloud | A pot-bellied man | A town council meeting
exercise: «The corporation voted to repair the park's swings.» In the UK, what does corporation mean here? | A football team | A school class | A big round belly | *The town council
exercise: The letters BBC stand for the British Broadcasting what? | Club | Council | Court | *Corporation
test: Grandad laughed so hard that his corporation wobbled. What does corporation mean here? | A large company | The town council | A group of workers | *A big round belly
test: Mia says a corporation is just a shop on the corner. Is she right? | *No, a corporation is a large company or group of companies that the law treats as one body | No, it is only a town council | Yes, every shop is a corporation | Yes, if the shop has a sign
exam: Why does context matter when you meet the word corporation? | It tells you how to spell the word | Context never helps with words that have more than one meaning | It means the same thing in every sentence | *It shows which meaning fits, such as a company, a town council or an old word for a belly
